","Chronic stress can significantly impair the functionality of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. NK cells are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing and killing abnormal cells, such as virus-infected or tumor cells. They are part of the innate immune system, meaning they can respond to threats without prior exposure.

When stress becomes chronic, it can lead to a significant reduction in NK cell function, making the immune system more susceptible to infections and potentially increasing the risk of cancer.

NK cell dysfunction in chronic stress can be observed in several ways:

