"What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by the formula:
\[
\Delta L = \frac{\lambda}{2 \pi \Delta n}
\]
where:
- \(\Delta L\) is the decay length,
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the wave,
- \(\Delta n\) is the difference between the refractive indices of the two media.

In this case, the core is GaAs and the cladding is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{.8}\)As. The difference in refractive indices is given as \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the core.

Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu\text{m} = 1000 \, \text{nm}\)
- \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\)

Substituting these values into the formula:
\[
\Delta L = \frac{1000 \, \text{nm}}{2 \pi (0.3)} = \frac{1000}{0.6 \pi \, \text{nm}} \approx \frac{1000}{1.96 \, \text{nm}} \approx 515 \, \text{nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is approximately \(\boxed{515 \, \text{nm}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
