","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton scattering formula:

\[
\lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} + \frac{2\lambda_{\text{scatt}}}{m_{\text{e}}} \cos \theta
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_{\text{scatt}}\) is the wavelength of the scattered photon.
- \(\lambda_{\text{scatt}}\) is the wavelength of the incident photon.
- \(m_{\text{e}}\) is the mass of the electron (9.1 \times 10^{-3} kg).
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when the scattering angle \(\theta = 0\) (head-on collision). In this case, the change in wavelength is:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} - \lambda_{\text{inc}} = \frac{2\lambda_{\text{inc}}}{m_{\text{e}}}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10}}{9.1 \times 10^{-3}} = 4.17 \times 10^{-7} \text{ Å}
\]

The percentage change in wavelength is given by:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_{\text{inc}}} \times 100\% = \frac{4.17 \times 10^{-7}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100\% = 20.85\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{20.85\%}\).

