","To determine the entropy production rate \( \Pi_S \), we use the Clausius inequality for irreversible processes:
\[ \pi_S = \frac{Q}{T} \geq 0 \]
where \( Q \) is the heat produced by the friction, and \( T \) is the room temperature.

In this case, the heat produced by the friction is due to the work done by the friction force. The work done by the friction force is equal to the friction force multiplied by the distance moved by the hand, which is proportional to the relative velocity \( v \) over time.

The work done by the friction force is:
\[ W = F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t \]
where \( \Delta t \) is the time interval.

The heat produced is equal to the work done by the friction force, so:
\[ Q = W = F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t \]

The entropy production rate is then:
\[ \pi_S = \frac{Q}{\Delta t T} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t}{\Delta t T} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T} \]

Thus, the entropy production rate is given by:
\[ \pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr} v}}{T} \]

The entropy production rate \( \pi_S \) is the rate at which entropy is produced by the irreversible process of friction.

